---
// promptanddone.com/filing: the FILING pack (DM deliverable for the "anthropic-s1-filing" reel).
import PackLayout from '../layouts/PackLayout.astro';
const prompt = "You are reading one part (of 8) of Anthropic's draft S-1 filing. Find the 3 facts in THIS part that a general AI-curious reader would find most surprising or revealing. For each, give an exact quote copied character-for-character from the text (one contiguous span, 80-300 characters, no ellipses, no edits). Output ONLY a JSON list of objects with keys \"fact\" (one plain sentence) and \"quote\".";
const checker = "#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check that quotes an AI pulled from a filing really appear in it. Usage: python3 check_quotes.py filing.txt quotes.json\nquotes.json = [{\"quote\": \"...\"}, ...]. Whitespace and curly quotes are normalised.\"\"\"\nimport json, re, sys\nnorm = lambda s: re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", s.replace(\"\\u2019\", \"'\").replace(\"\\u201c\", '\"').replace(\"\\u201d\", '\"').replace(\"\\u2018\", \"'\")).strip()\ntext = norm(open(sys.argv[1], encoding=\"utf8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())\nqs = json.load(open(sys.argv[2])); hits = 0\nfor i, q in enumerate(qs, 1):\n    ok = norm(q[\"quote\"]) in text; hits += ok; print(i, \"FOUND\" if ok else \"NOT FOUND\")\nprint(f\"{hits}/{len(qs)} found word for word\")\n";
---
<PackLayout title="Anthropic's S-1, read by Claude: the FILING pack — Prompt &amp; Done">
  <main>
    <header>
      <p class="kicker"><a href="/">Prompt &amp; Done</a> · FILING pack</p>
      <h1>Anthropic's S-1: a $42.0 billion loss, and what it is made of</h1>
      <p class="tag">A filing, not advice. Numbers below are copied from the S-1 as filed on 26 Oct 2026; amounts in the filing's statements are in millions of dollars. This is a registration statement that will change: the offering price range, share counts and Run-Rate Revenue are blank in it, so we say nothing about them.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">1</span> The numbers in the reel</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Net loss 2025: <b>$42.0 billion</b> ($41,965 million). Risk factor "We have a history of net losses", page 63 of the filing.</li>
        <li>Of that, <b>$34.3 billion</b> is the change in fair value of convertible notes, derivative liabilities and SAFEs ($34,308 million), "primarily driven by an increase in the fair value of our outstanding convertible promissory notes resulting from an increase in our enterprise valuation" (pages 63-64, and the summary table on page 22). It is a fair-value change, not cash paid out.</li>
        <li>Operating loss 2025: <b>$8,060 million</b> on revenue of <b>$4,585 million</b>; the operating costs include a $1,500 million "Bartz settlement" line. Interest income of $431 million is not shown in the reel's coin tower.</li>
        <li>Six months to 30 June 2026: revenue <b>$16,286 million</b>, operating loss <b>$693 million</b> (page 22).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Source: <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/2130357/000162828026067168/anthropicpbc-sx1.htm">sec.gov, Anthropic, PBC Form S-1</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="card"><h2><span class="n">2</span> The test we ran (26 Oct 2026)</h2>
      <p>We split the filing text (about 1.57 million characters) into 8 equal parts and asked Claude Sonnet, one call per part, for the 3 most surprising facts with an exact quote. All 8 calls ran in parallel in about 29 seconds (about $2.53 API-equivalent). Then we searched every quote in the filing text: <b>24 of 24 were there word for word</b>. Claude's one-line summaries sometimes add to the quote, so trust the quote and read it in the filing.</p>
      <pre><code>{prompt}</code></pre>
      <details><summary>check_quotes.py</summary><pre><code>{checker}</code></pre></details></section>
